## Preliminaries: prepare a learning experiment

Choose the backend once. The notebook-only `learn` function passes visible
Prolog source to the shared integration helper. That helper starts a session,
loads the learner and this task, performs bounded learning, executes the
returned clauses, and closes its session in `after`.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
project = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)
Code.require_file(Path.join(project, "examples/ilp.exs"))
backend = :port

learn = fn task, budget, source, selected ->
  ILPShowcase.learn(:metagol, task, budget, selected, %{"metagol_tasks.pl" => source})
end
```

Change `backend` to `:nif` to use embedded SWI through the same interface.
Metagol's registry and native modules are shared, so run learning experiments
serially in a runtime. The adapter restores the settings and primitive
registrations it changes. The final comparison runs both backends for you.

## Understand the learning contract

Inductive Logic Programming seeks a hypothesis **H** from background knowledge
**B**, positive examples **E+**, negative examples **E−**, and a language bias.
A hypothesis must explain the positives and reject the negatives within that
bias. Passing a small dataset does not prove the hypothesis is universally right.

```elixir
Kino.Mermaid.new("""
flowchart LR
  B["Background predicates"] --> M["Metagol search"]
  E["Positive and negative examples"] --> M
  R["Metarules and clause budget"] --> M
  M --> H["Learned Prolog clauses"]
  H --> T["Execute on withheld queries"]
""")
```

Metarules constrain the shape of candidate clauses. The shared adapter declares
these four upstream forms; predicate symbols themselves are learned.

| Template       | Allowed clause              | Purpose                     |
| -------------- | --------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Identity       | `P(A,B) :- Q(A,B).`         | Reuse a primitive           |
| Chain          | `P(A,B) :- Q(A,C), R(C,B).` | Compose relations           |
| Tail recursion | `P(A,B) :- Q(A,C), P(C,B).` | Follow paths                |
| Higher-order   | `P(A,B) :- Q(A,B,F).`       | Learn an argument predicate |

The task source below selects available primitives and supplies training and
test cases. Tests are used only after learning; their labels do not constrain
the learner. Every displayed program is returned by Metagol, converted to
ordinary clauses, and executed by SWI.

## Invent a missing concept

### What you will learn

Learn a relation that needs a reusable intermediate predicate. The task supplies
`mg_mother/2` and `mg_father/2`, but no `parent/2`. Five positives cover paths
through both kinds of parent; `grandparent(amy,amelia)` is negative.

This is the complete executable task. The Nora–Ivy–Leo facts support a
project-authored holdout query that never appears among the training labels.

```elixir
grandparent_source = ~S"""
:- module(metagol_tasks, [primitives/2, training/3, tests/2]).

mg_mother(ann,amy). mg_mother(ann,andy). mg_mother(amy,amelia).
mg_mother(linda,gavin). mg_mother(nora,ivy).
mg_father(steve,amy). mg_father(steve,andy). mg_father(gavin,amelia).
mg_father(andy,spongebob). mg_father(ivy,leo).

primitives(grandparent, [mg_mother/2,mg_father/2]).

training(grandparent,
 [grandparent(ann,amelia),grandparent(steve,amelia),grandparent(ann,spongebob),
  grandparent(steve,spongebob),grandparent(linda,amelia)],
 [grandparent(amy,amelia)]).

tests(grandparent,
 [grandparent(nora,leo)-true, grandparent(ann,gavin)-false,
  grandparent(ann,ann)-false]).
"""
```

Before running, consider how to cover all mother/father combinations with
three clauses. What concept would let you reuse the same relation twice?

```elixir
grandparent = learn.(:grandparent, 3, grandparent_source, backend) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
assert grandparent["Status"] == {:atom, "learned"}
assert grandparent["Clauses"] == 3
Kino.Text.new(grandparent["Program"])
```

An invented predicate, usually named `grandparent_1`, plays the role of
**parent**. The name was not supplied as a primitive. Check both training
and withheld queries in the actual execution report.

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new(grandparent["Rows"], keys: [:split, :goal, :expected, :actual])
```

### Restrict the program budget

Metagol deepens its search by clause count. A budget changes which programs
are expressible; it is not a number of training iterations.

```elixir
budgets =
  for budget <- [2, 3] do
    result = learn.(:grandparent, budget, grandparent_source, backend)
    expected = if budget == 2, do: "no_hypothesis", else: "learned"
    assert result["Status"] == {:atom, expected}
    %{budget: budget, status: expected, clauses: result["Clauses"]}
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(budgets)
```

`no_hypothesis` means this bounded search found no consistent program in the
configured space. A timeout raises `SWI.Error` and is an unknown outcome.

### Try it yourself

Remove the withheld Nora–Ivy–Leo query from `tests/2`, leaving training unchanged.
Predict whether this changes the learned program or only its evaluation report.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

Test labels are used only after learning. The hypothesis and training scores
should remain the same, while the report has one fewer test row. Update your
expected test count if you add a check for it.

</details>

## Learn recursion

A two-clause ancestor program must cover both one edge and paths of arbitrary
length. This task supplies parent edges and three positive ancestor queries;
there is no ancestor implementation in the background.

```elixir
ancestor_source = ~S"""
:- module(metagol_tasks, [primitives/2, training/3, tests/2]).

mg_parent(queen_victoria,edward_seventh).
mg_parent(edward_seventh,george_fifth).
mg_parent(george_fifth,george_sixth).
mg_parent(george_sixth,elizabeth_second).
mg_parent(queen_mother,elizabeth_second).
mg_parent(elizabeth_second,prince_charles).
mg_parent(prince_philip,prince_charles).
mg_parent(prince_charles,prince_william).
mg_parent(prince_charles,prince_harry).
mg_parent(princess_diana,prince_william).
mg_parent(princess_diana,prince_harry).
mg_parent(prince_william,prince_george).

primitives(ancestor, [mg_parent/2]).

training(ancestor,
 [ancestor(elizabeth_second,prince_charles),ancestor(george_sixth,prince_harry),
  ancestor(queen_mother,prince_william)], []).

tests(ancestor,
 [ancestor(queen_victoria,prince_george)-true,
  ancestor(princess_diana,prince_george)-true,
  ancestor(prince_george,queen_victoria)-false, ancestor(queen_victoria,queen_victoria)-false]).
"""
```

The Queen Victoria–Prince George test path is longer than the training paths.
Reverse and self queries catch overly permissive hypotheses. These identifiers
form a toy graph, not a complete genealogy.

```elixir
ancestor = learn.(:ancestor, 2, ancestor_source, backend) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
assert ancestor["Clauses"] == 2
Kino.Text.new(ancestor["Program"])
```

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new(Enum.filter(ancestor["Rows"], &(&1.split == "test")),
  keys: [:goal, :expected, :actual]
)
```

## Transform nested lists

Now learn how to remove the last item from **each inner list**. The available
primitives are tail and reverse. The adapter provides the upstream interpreted
`map` combinator, allowing Metagol to learn the predicate mapped over each list.
No drop-last implementation is supplied.

```elixir
droplasts_source = ~S"""
:- module(metagol_tasks, [primitives/2, training/3, tests/2]).



primitives(droplasts, [mg_tail/2,mg_reverse/2]).

training(droplasts,
 [droplasts([[a,b,c],[d,e,f,g]],[[a,b],[d,e,f]]),
  droplasts([[h,i],[j,k,l],[m,n,o,p]],[[h],[j,k],[m,n,o]])],
 [droplasts([[a,b,c]],[[b,c]])]).

tests(droplasts,
 [droplasts([[q],[r,s,t,u,v],[]],[[],[r,s,t,u],[]])-false,
  droplasts([[q],[r,s,t,u,v]],[[],[r,s,t,u]])-true,
  droplasts([],[])-true, droplasts([[x,y]],[[y]])-false]).
"""
```

Predict the composition that converts `[a,b,c]` to `[a,b]` using only reverse
and tail. The learned program must also apply that composition to every sublist.

```elixir
droplasts = learn.(:droplasts, 3, droplasts_source, backend) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
assert droplasts["Clauses"] == 3
Kino.Text.new(droplasts["Program"])
```

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new(droplasts["Rows"], keys: [:split, :goal, :expected, :actual])
```

The tests include a singleton inner list, a longer unseen list, an empty outer
list, and a tempting wrong output. Empty **inner** lists lie outside this
transformation's domain because tail fails on `[]`; the negative case checks
that behavior explicitly.

## Compare backends

First check each learned program against its labels, then compare Port and NIF.
Agreement alone would allow two identical mistakes to pass. Inference counts
and times include setup within the learning query and evaluation; they are not
measurements of the learner alone.

```elixir
comparisons =
  for {task, budget, source} <- [
        {:grandparent, 3, grandparent_source},
        {:ancestor, 2, ancestor_source},
        {:droplasts, 3, droplasts_source}
      ] do
    port = learn.(task, budget, source, :port) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
    nif = learn.(task, budget, source, :nif) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
    assert port["Rows"] == nif["Rows"]
    assert port["Clauses"] == nif["Clauses"]

    %{
      task: task,
      clauses: port["Clauses"],
      matching_predictions: true,
      port_inferences: port["Inferences"],
      nif_inferences: nif["Inferences"]
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(comparisons)
```

## Session cleanup

Every `learn` call closes its session before returning. Learned clauses are
removed after evaluation; returned program text and row maps remain usable.
Reevaluate a task cell and its learning cell to try a new dataset. Worked
examples check their stated answers, so update those checks for experiments
that deliberately change the labels or budget.

## Choose your next step

Try removing the negative droplasts example and designing a new withheld case
that distinguishes the intended transformation from a spurious hypothesis.
Print `droplasts["Trace"]` to inspect Metagol's search history.

